{:edb/db-before-t 274 :edb/db-after-t 275 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533587 50 #inst "2026-10-11T10:43:45.150Z" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161754 1071 "![](images/5c/5c5e52c78f925dc4e01b1098b507865407f9848cc1db6e8a946ad81840e8e4ee.png)\n\n  \n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X \\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(2) = 4$\n\n- $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y$\n\n- The function $f:X \\to Y$ is **into**" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161754 1071 "![](images/5c/5c5e52c78f925dc4e01b1098b507865407f9848cc1db6e8a946ad81840e8e4ee.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X \\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n1. $f(2) = 4$\n2. $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$\n3. The function $f:X \\to Y$ is **into**" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186161754 1074 "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, there is an arrow from $2 \\in X$ to $4\\in Y$. So, $f(2)=4$.\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $1 \\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $1 \\in Y$. As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=1$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x) \\neq y$ for any $x$ in $X$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186161754 1074 "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed,   there is an arrow from $2 \\in X$ to $4\\in Y.$ So,  $f(2)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $1 \\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $1 \\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=1$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x) \\neq y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161755 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^2-2$?\n\n1. The range of $f$ is $[-2, \\infty)$\n2. $\\forall\\, y \\in [-2, \\infty)$, $\\exists\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)=y$\n3. $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ **into** $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186161755 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^2-2?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[-2, \\infty)$\n\n- $\\forall\\, y \\in [-2, \\infty),$ $\\exists\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\n- $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ **into** $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161755 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35949b4a4809821871a9edf9466fa00862778094d754674014407525b5fc2fed.png)\n\nRecall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2$, and it occurs at the point $(1,-2)$. The graph will cover all $y$-values higher than $y=-2$, including $y=-2$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $[-2, \\infty)$.\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[-2, \\infty)$, then for all $y$ contained within $[-2, \\infty)$, there exists some $x$ contained within $\\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x) = y$.\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$. So, $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ *into* $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186161755 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35949b4a4809821871a9edf9466fa00862778094d754674014407525b5fc2fed.png)\n\nRecall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2,$ and it occurs at the point $(1,-2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values higher than $y=-2$, including $y=-2$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $[-2, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[-2, \\infty)$, then for all $y$ contained within $[-2, \\infty),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $\\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x) = y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ So, $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ *into* $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161756 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f: [-2, \\infty) \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) =\\sqrt{ x+2}$\n\n- $g: \\mathbb{R} \\to [0, \\infty)$ defined by $g(x) = |x-2|+3$\n\n- $h:(0,\\infty) \\to\\Bbb R$ defined by $h(x) = \\ln(x)$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161756 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n1. $f: [-2, \\infty) \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) =\\sqrt{x+2}$\n2. $g: \\mathbb{R} \\to [0, \\infty)$ defined by $g(x) = \\mid x-2 \\mid +3$\n3. $h:(0,\\infty) \\to\\Bbb R$ defined by $h(x) = \\ln(x)$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186161756 1074 "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1 \\in\\Bbb R$. There is no number $x \\in[-2, \\infty)$ such that $f(x) =\\sqrt{x+2}=-1$. In fact, $f(x) =\\sqrt{x+2} \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in[-2, \\infty)$.\n- II is an into function. Let's take $1 \\in [0, \\infty)$. There is no number $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $g(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid +3=1$. In fact, $\\mid x-2 \\mid +3 \\geq 3$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n- III is a *not* an into function. Given any $y \\in \\Bbb R$, we can choose $x =e^{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $h(x) = h \\left(e^{y}\\right) =\\ln(e^{y}) = y$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186161756 1074 "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1 \\in\\Bbb R.$ There is no number $x \\in[-2, \\infty)$ such that $f(x) =\\sqrt{ x+2}=-1.$ In fact, $f(x) =\\sqrt{ x+2}  \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in[-2, \\infty) .$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $1 \\in [0, \\infty).$ There is no number $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$  such that $g(x)=|x-2|+3=1.$ In fact, $|x-2|+3 \\geq 3$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- III is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y \\in \\Bbb R,$ we can choose $x =e^{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Then $h(x) = h \\left( e^{y}\\right) =\\ln(e^{y}) = y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410007 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410013 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410015 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410006 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410009 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410011 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161922 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410008 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161922 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410010 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161922 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410012 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186161922 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410014 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162180 1050 480.0 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158612 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186162235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409998 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162235 1071 "![](images/b3/b3c5df109b00450c20acdbc07378bfe309cf808c1244bedd6ff4fa3a850ccd13.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f: X → Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n1. $f(10) = 25$\n2. $∃y ∈ Y$ such that $∀x ∈ X$, we have $f(x) \\ne y$\n3. The function $f: X → Y$ is not **into**" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186162235 1071 "![](images/b3/b3c5df109b00450c20acdbc07378bfe309cf808c1244bedd6ff4fa3a850ccd13.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(10)=25$\n- $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n- The function $f:X\\to Y$ is not **into**" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162235 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed,   there is an arrow from $10\\in X$ to $25\\in Y.$ So,  $f(10)=25.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160694 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186162567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409997 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162567 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={5}^{x}$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162567 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n1. $f: R → (0, ∞)$ defined by $f(x) = 2^{x}$\n2. $g: Q → Q$ defined by $g(x) = x^{4}$\n3. $h: R → R$ defined by $h(x) = 5^{x}$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186162567 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{2}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{2}⁡y)={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfy $g(x)={x}^{4}=2$ are $x=\\pm \\sqrt[4]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={5}^{x}=-1.$ In fact, ${5}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160700 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186162568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409993 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162568 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f: R → R$ which is given by $f(x) = 3^{x}$?\n\n1. The range of $f$ is $(0, ∞)$\n2. $∀y ∈ R$, $∃x ∈ R$ such that $f(x) = y$\n3. $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186162568 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={3}^{x}?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186162568 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/a2/a2a6352a60b420f8721d6c7e4553d3157cfb045a39b6a5119c11c5c8189023e2.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can' t be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186163212 1064 "A function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is said to be an  **into** function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write: \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nThe following diagram shows an example of an into function. \n\n![](images/ff/ff2c2b4099fc1cf30beb2d0f795049e55ad955b06a298d0bd93d8724e22cd0e3.png)\n\n  \n\n According to the diagram,  the element $4 \\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $4.$So, we conclude that there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=4$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x) \\neq y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\nOn the other hand, the following is *not* an into function because every element in $Y$ has at least one element of $X$ that maps to it. \n\n![](images/12/120185557c1f1e2846dece02a4675c2952d1e6c3a01cd66a17458596302266ac.png)" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186163212 1064 "A function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is said to be an **into** function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$.\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nThe following diagram shows an example of an into function.\n\n![](images/ff/ff2c2b4099fc1cf30beb2d0f795049e55ad955b06a298d0bd93d8724e22cd0e3.png)\n\nAccording to the diagram, the element $4 \\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $4$.So, we conclude that there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=4$) contained within $Y$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\neq y\n$$\n\nfor any $x$ in $X$.\n\nOn the other hand, the following is *not* an into function because every element in $Y$ has at least one element of $X$ that maps to it.\n\n![](images/12/120185557c1f1e2846dece02a4675c2952d1e6c3a01cd66a17458596302266ac.png)" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186163213 1064 "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nConsider the function $f: \\Bbb R \\rightarrow [0,\\infty)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\mid x-1 \\mid +1\n$$\n\nNotice that the codomain is not $\\mathbb R$ but $[0,\\infty) \\subseteq \\mathbb R$. Let's show that $f$ is an into function.\n\nTo show that $f$ is into $[0,\\infty)$, let's take ${\\color{blue}0} \\in [0, \\infty)$. Notice that there is no number $x$ in the domain $\\mathbb{R}$ such that\n\n$$\ng(x)= \\mid x-1 \\mid +1={\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n\nIn fact,\n\n$$\n\\mid x-1 \\mid +1 \\geq 1\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThus, we have showed that $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ (e.g.,\n\n$$\ny={\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n\n) such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) \\neq y\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ is an into function.\n\nThe graph of our function $f$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/110203c5a05e9e1b56bbd8483bd78e1be9245a7f1b73738e22ce469ce44f3af1.png)" 13194139533587 17592186045417 false] [17592186163213 1064 "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$\n\nConsider the function $f: \\Bbb R \\rightarrow  [0,\\infty)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=|x-1|+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the codomain is not $\\mathbb R$ but $[0,\\infty) \\subseteq \\mathbb R.$ Let's show that $f$ is an into function.  \n\nTo show that $f$ is into $[0,\\infty),$ let's take ${\\color{blue}0} \\in [0, \\infty).$ Notice that there is no number $x$ in the domain $\\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\ng(x)=|x-1|+1={\\color{blue}0}.\n$$\n\n \nIn fact, $|x-1|+1 \\geq 1$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$      \n\nThus, we have showed that $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ (e.g., $y={\\color{blue}0}$) such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X,$ we have $f(x) \\neq y.$ Therefore, $f$ is an into function. \n\nThe graph of our function $f$  is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/110203c5a05e9e1b56bbd8483bd78e1be9245a7f1b73738e22ce469ce44f3af1.png)" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186409928 1080 "I and II only" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186409928 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186409928 1083 #uuid "05cadc09-da0d-5ae7-aeaf-a7c3e35f4b2c" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186409929 1080 "I only" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186409929 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186409929 1083 #uuid "07dec380-033c-5dbf-a5f5-d2f352afa594" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186409930 1080 "I only" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186409930 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533587 17592186045417