13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410006 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/3575afe4fd4e5aa6754496154986b272a43fb5c035ec8d69621d52fad16ee1ea.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $2,$ and it occurs at the point $(0,2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values lower than $y=2$, including $y=2$ itself. So the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2]$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,2],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=3\\in R.$ So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409999 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410007 1069 "q-105231" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410007 1071 "![](images/37/373448202bfd022f5629219b2c176f4096e4eeae275c1095798d77be92122cc2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(1)=4$\n- $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n- The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410007 1072 #uuid "1346b272-ab34-50a3-8e2c-0cbfe3ad7ce0" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410007 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $2\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)=2\\ne 4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $4\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $4\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=4$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409994 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410008 1069 "q-90878" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410008 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $g:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$\n- $h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410008 1072 #uuid "2021b7c7-5d84-5929-874c-8d6923b11db1" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410008 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f(\\sqrt[3]{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{3}=y.$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={e}^{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g({e}^{y})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y})=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={e}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409996 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410009 1069 "q-105258" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410009 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={2}^{-x}?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[0,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410009 1072 #uuid "39b57e8a-5e0f-5758-8d6b-0d9667e7e35b" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410009 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/c7/c79c1123036c978c8c4da514651c0bb1c8e3df34c8bbaff099bee601d568ed10.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph will cover all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410003 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410010 1069 "q-90955" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410010 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$\n- $g:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- $h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410010 1072 #uuid "3fb6cc00-0fe3-5564-afaa-6bddf30c9f68" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410010 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}=-1.$ In fact, $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in [0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g(\\sqrt{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{2}=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={e}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410001 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410011 1069 "q-90859" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410011 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R$ $∃\\,x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ maps $(0,\\infty )$ **into** $R$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410011 1072 #uuid "4110bb56-279f-50bf-96a1-301ebf1eb3ce" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410011 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3e074a2d029aeb3f4016332b9fb762fff6ec60ed7dce0290f97a95d6cb57c1.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, there is no highest or lowest $y$ value. The graph will cover any real value $y.$ So the range of the function is all $R..$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$ the function is a surjection. Therefore, $f$ is *not* an into function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409995 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410012 1069 "q-105263" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410012 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to (-\\infty ,1)$ which is given by $f(x)=1-{2}^{x}?$\n\n- The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,1)$\n- $∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,1],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a function of $R$ **into** $(-\\infty ,1)$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410012 1072 #uuid "9b62af08-9109-5599-b006-f2ceb899c9fa" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410012 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35bd21eef06d1ea63aba6e420d9842de61f9248c4fa26c2895f6825f4e7a3848.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values lower than $y=1$, excluding $y=1$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1)$,  there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $1\\notin (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is true, the function is a surjection.  So, $f$ is *not* a function of $R$ *into* $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410000 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410013 1069 "q-90789" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410013 1071 "![](images/7f/7f68e70397b982a70b372b2bd9e4f73da3374d3abfb2ef4bbd2ec1fffe6dbb31.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(2)=4$\n- $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n- The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410013 1072 #uuid "ae359058-a9c8-58ca-9940-1c14f3b8e228" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410013 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   there is an arrow from $2\\in X$ to $4\\in Y.$ So,  $f(2)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410005 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410014 1069 "q-90902" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410014 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-{log}_{2}⁡(x)$\n- $h:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={3}^{x}$\n- $g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410014 1072 #uuid "d76a2452-4488-5b99-ace5-042b8edc218d" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410014 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={2}^{-y}\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Then $f(x)=f({2}^{-y})=-{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{-y})=y.$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y)}=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $y=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}=y.$ Indeed, in this case we would have \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x}=y\\,⇔\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\,⇔\\,x=\\sqrt{2}\\in Q,\n$$\n\n which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410002 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410015 1069 "q-105246" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410015 1071 "![](images/ae/ae980a05fc0d25d2696185c88fcf90ff156af9c3f42af7cece80f8c33692a3d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(1)=7$\n- $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n- The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410015 1072 #uuid "e45af0c1-a25f-5254-88c0-adcdabddd288" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true] [17592186410015 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is no arrow from $1\\in X$ to $7\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)\\ne 7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $5\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $5\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=5$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533587 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-05cadc09-da0d-5ae7-aeaf-a7c3e35f4b2c" 17592186409928 "answer-07dec380-033c-5dbf-a5f5-d2f352afa594" 17592186409929 "answer-0af2f699-18f8-5488-b57d-5bedd5d91355" 17592186409930 "answer-0c43997e-0ae2-54ee-8b77-f5d270f7bf43" 17592186409931 "answer-0e10e89a-3526-5277-97f0-adb1bf3a9d69" 17592186409932 "answer-0ee280af-f80a-5d65-a83c-f42a485b1769" 17592186409933 "answer-14fbca50-5fc0-5de1-bc35-ddb30ff5e841" 17592186409934 "answer-16e59bd1-b50e-5bf9-aa10-849f69270ddf" 